<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hurricane Ivan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hurricane Ivan was a powerful Atlantic hurricane that struck the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in September 2004. On 7 September it passed over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, damaging or destroying about 90 per cent of the island's buildings and killing dozens of people, then went on to hit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, the Cayman Islands and the US Gulf coast. It caused about 90 deaths in all.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hurricane-ivan.jpg" alt="Hurricane Ivan" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA image courtesy Jacques Descloitres, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team at NASA GSFC. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_2004-09-13_1900Z_(alternate).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Ivan grew from a tropical wave that left western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and became a tropical depression on 2 September 2004 south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a>. A day later the National Hurricane Center named it Tropical Storm Ivan, the ninth named storm of the 2004 Atlantic hurricane season, and on 3 September it became the southernmost Atlantic hurricane on record, at 9.5° N. It reached hurricane strength on 5 September and intensified rapidly into a major hurricane.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 7 September Ivan crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a> with winds of 125 mph. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, its worst hurricane since Janet in 1955, about 90% of houses were damaged, some 18,000 people were left homeless, and the damage, $1.1 billion, came to about 200% of the country's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>; 80% of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> crop, its main agricultural export, was lost. In the Caribbean Sea Ivan reached Category 5 three times, with peak winds of 165 mph, and passed just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, where 17 people died, and of the Cayman Islands.</p><p>On 16 September Ivan came ashore at Gulf Shores, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, as a Category 3 hurricane with sustained winds of 120 mph. Its storm surge, which peaked at 12.92 ft at the Escambia Bay Bridge, destroyed parts of the bridge carrying Interstate 10, and 27 people were killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. Moving inland it set off about 120 tornadoes from Alabama to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, the largest outbreak linked to a tropical cyclone. Its remnant drifted back over Florida, regenerated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> and came ashore in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> as a tropical depression before dissipating on 24 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Damage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> came to $20.5 billion, making Ivan the country's second-costliest hurricane at the time after Andrew in 1992. Waves destroyed Taylor Energy's Mississippi Canyon 20-A oil platform off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, and oil was still leaking there fourteen years later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a> and the United States Agency for International Development sent aid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, and the Caribbean Development Bank lent it $10 million in 2005. The World Meteorological Organization retired the name Ivan in 2005, replacing it with Igor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hurricane Ivan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Keith Mitchell served as prime minister for many years, <b>Hurricane Ivan</b> wrecked the island in 2004, and Dickon Mitchell took office in 2022.</blockquote><a href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a></li></ul></section>
